      "id": 1,
      "text": "William Glenn first settled what would become Curdsville in 1797, building a log cabin near Panther Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curdsville%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1848, Curdsville was officially laid out, marking its transition from a frontier settlement to a planned town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kyatlas.com/ky-curdsville.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s ferry across the Green River began operation in 1855, connecting Curdsville to communities upriver.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wiki.historyofowensboro.com/index.php?title=Curdsville,_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Curdsville’s post office served residents from 1855 until its closure in 2010, spanning over 150 years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kyatlas.com/ky-curdsville.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Historically, Curdsville thrived as a river port, trading goods between Owensboro and the Green River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kyatlas.com/ky-curdsville.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Located 13 miles west of Owensboro, Curdsville sits at the mouth of Panther Creek on the Green River.",
